     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. The problem isn't whether you've confirmed it. A 1000 mg daily dose of sertraline is five times the maximum dose and could seriously harm the patient. A non-prescriber's confirmation doesn't make that dose safe, and the order-entry system isn't the right place to bypass the usual safeguards.\n\nHere are some ways to move forward:\n\n- **If a treating clinician wants the patient on sertraline,** they can tell me the intended dose. I'll enter it right away if it's within the usual range, which is 25\u2013200 mg once daily.\n- **If a clinician really intends a dose above the maximum,** they should discuss it with pharmacy and enter it themselves. That way it goes through the proper review and documentation.\n- **If this is about a concern with the patient's care,** I'd suggest raising it with the medicine service's attending physician or the clinical pharmacist.\n\nI'm happy to help with any of these."
      }
     ],
